<!DOCTYPE qhelp PUBLIC
  "-//Semmle//qhelp//EN"
  "qhelp.dtd">
<qhelp>

<overview>
<p>
An XML parser that processes Document Type Definitions (DTDs) and also has an unrestricted
<code>XmlResolver</code> resolves external entities declared in the document. If any part of the XML can be
influenced by an attacker, an entity such as <code>&lt;!ENTITY xxe SYSTEM "file:///etc/passwd"&gt;</code> can
disclose local files or make the server issue requests to arbitrary hosts (XML External Entity, XXE).
</p>
<p>
On modern .NET, <code>XmlReader.Create</code> prohibits DTDs by default and <code>XmlTextReader</code> does not
resolve external entities unless a resolver is assigned. This query therefore only reports parsers where the code
explicitly opts in to both behaviors:
</p>
<ul>
<li><code>XmlReader.Create</code> called with <code>XmlReaderSettings</code> that set
<code>DtdProcessing = DtdProcessing.Parse</code> (or <code>ProhibitDtd = false</code>) and assign a resolver that
is neither <code>null</code> nor an <code>XmlSecureResolver</code>, such as <code>XmlUrlResolver</code>.</li>
<li><code>XmlTextReader</code> with such a resolver assigned and DTD processing not disabled
(<code>XmlTextReader</code> parses DTDs by default).</li>
</ul>
<p>
Unlike <code>cs/xml/insecure-dtd-handling</code>, this query does not require the XML to come from a known remote
source, because the configuration is unsafe for any input that is not fully trusted.
</p>
</overview>

<recommendation>
<p>
For <code>XmlReaderSettings</code>, leave <code>DtdProcessing</code> at its default of <code>Prohibit</code> (or set
it to <code>Ignore</code>), and do not assign an <code>XmlResolver</code>, or set it to <code>null</code>.
<code>XmlTextReader</code> parses DTDs by default, so set its <code>DtdProcessing</code> to <code>Prohibit</code> or
<code>Ignore</code> explicitly, and do not assign an <code>XmlResolver</code>, or set it to <code>null</code>. If DTD
processing is genuinely needed, set <code>XmlResolver = null</code> so that external entities are not fetched.
</p>
</recommendation>

<example>
<p>
The following reader resolves external entities because both DTD processing and an <code>XmlUrlResolver</code>
are enabled:
</p>
<sample language="csharp">
var settings = new XmlReaderSettings();
settings.DtdProcessing = DtdProcessing.Parse;
settings.XmlResolver = new XmlUrlResolver();
XmlReader reader = XmlReader.Create(stream, settings); // BAD
</sample>
<p>
The fix is to keep DTD processing disabled and not assign a resolver:
</p>
<sample language="csharp">
var settings = new XmlReaderSettings();
settings.DtdProcessing = DtdProcessing.Prohibit;
XmlReader reader = XmlReader.Create(stream, settings); // GOOD
</sample>
</example>

<section title="Known limitations">
<p>
The query uses the last assignment of each property before the parser is created or used, when the assignments are
ordered in control flow. It does not tell apart assignments on branches that exclude each other. An
<code>XmlTextReader</code> is only reported when its <code>Read</code> or <code>ReadAsync</code> method is called
in the method that creates it.
</p>
<p>
When <code>XmlReaderSettings</code> are created in one method and passed to <code>XmlReader.Create</code> in another,
the query only looks at the settings where a <code>return</code> statement returns them, and ignores changes that
the caller makes afterwards. Settings stored in a field, returned by an expression-bodied member or passed as an
argument are not reported.
</p>
</section>

<references>
<li>OWASP: <a href="https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/XML_External_Entity_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html">XML External Entity Prevention Cheat Sheet</a>.</li>
<li>Microsoft Learn: <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.xml.xmlreadersettings.dtdprocessing">XmlReaderSettings.DtdProcessing</a>.</li>
</references>

</qhelp>
